.TH LAN\-SCAN 1 "2026-10-29" "mu2edaq-cluster-tools 1.0.1" "Mu2e DAQ Manual"
.SH NAME
lan\-scan \- probe a local network and list responding hosts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lan\-scan
.RB [ \-n
.IR CIDR ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR SECONDS ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR COUNT ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR PER_SECOND ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-hosts
.IR COUNT ]
.RB [ \-\-allow\-large ]
.RB [ \-\-allow\-routable ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR LIST ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-dns ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B lan\-scan
.BR \-\-version | \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lan-scan
discovers hosts on a network by sending an ICMP ping (and,
optionally, TCP connect probes) to every address in a subnet, then
prints a table of the hosts that responded along with their reverse-DNS
name and IP address. Progress
.RB ( "Scanning <cidr> (<n> addresses, <w> workers, rate <r>/s)..." )
and notes go to standard error; results go to standard output.
.PP
If no network is given, the local IPv4 networks are detected from
the host's interfaces (\fBip addr show\fR on Linux, \fBifconfig\fR on
macOS/BSD, \fBipconfig\fR on Windows). Loopback (127/8) and link-local
(169.254/16) networks are dropped and duplicates merged. If exactly one
network remains it is scanned and named on standard error together with the
interface and local address it came from, e.g.
.BR "note: auto-detected 10.226.9.0/24 on interface eth0 (10.226.9.5)" .
If more than one remains (a multi-homed DAQ node, a container bridge, a VPN)
.B lan-scan
does not guess: it lists every candidate (sorted by network address, with
interface name and address) and exits with status 1; re-run with
.BR \-\-network .
An auto-detected network is still subject to the size and routability
limits below.
.SH PROBES AND SAFETY
These tools run on production DAQ networks, so the defaults are deliberately
conservative.
.PP
.B Size limit.
A network with more probe addresses than
.B \-\-max\-hosts
(default 1024) is refused with exit status 3 unless
.B \-\-allow\-large
is given. A /22 (1022 addresses) passes; a /21 or a mistyped /16 does not.
.PP
.B Routable address space.
A network that is globally routable (Python
.BR ipaddress.is_global :
not RFC 1918, CGNAT, loopback, link-local or other reserved space) is refused
with exit status 3 unless
.B \-\-allow\-routable
is given. A sweep of site address space can be indistinguishable from
reconnaissance to the site's intrusion detection; only scan it if you are
authorised to. The shipped
.I config/lan\-scan/lan_scan\-mu2edaq.yaml
targets
.BR 131.225.245.0/24 ,
a routable Fermilab subnet, and therefore requires
.B \-\-allow\-routable
on every run.
.PP
.B Rate and concurrency.
At most
.B \-\-workers
(default 64, maximum 256) addresses are probed at once, and at most
.B \-\-rate
(default 50) new addresses are started per second across all workers
.RB ( 0
disables the rate limit). Addresses are generated lazily; no list of the
whole network is built. At the defaults a /24 takes at least about 5 s
(254 addresses at 50/s).
.PP
For every probe address
.RB ( ipaddress.hosts() ,
i.e. excluding the network and broadcast addresses of anything larger than
a /31; all addresses of a /31 or /32), one worker thread:
.IP 1. 4
runs the system
.BR ping (8)
once as a subprocess with the reply wait set to the timeout, in the unit
each platform expects:
.RS
.TP
Linux (iputils, BusyBox)
.B ping \-c 1 \-W
.IR seconds ,
rounded up to whole seconds (minimum 1), because older iputils accept
integers only;
.TP
macOS, FreeBSD
.B ping \-c 1 \-W
.IR milliseconds ;
.TP
Windows
.B ping \-n 1 \-w
.IR milliseconds .
.RE
.IP
The subprocess is killed 0.5 s after the requested wait if it has not exited;
.IP 2. 4
only if the ping failed and
.B \-\-ports
is set, attempts a full TCP connect to each listed port in turn
.RI ( timeout
seconds each), closing the connection as soon as one succeeds;
.IP 3. 4
only for a responder, and unless
.BR \-\-no\-dns ,
does one reverse lookup with
.BR gethostbyaddr (3)
in a separate thread, waiting at most
.I timeout
seconds for it; a slower lookup is abandoned and the host is reported
without a name. No process-global socket timeout is changed.
.PP
Nothing is sent other than the ICMP echo, the TCP SYN (and handshake) and
the DNS query; no payload is written to an open port.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-network\fR \fICIDR\fR
Network to scan, e.g. \fB192.168.1.0/24\fR. Host bits are ignored.
Default: auto-detect (see DESCRIPTION).
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR
YAML configuration file supplying defaults for any of the options below.
See \fIconfig/lan\-scan/lan_scan.yaml.example\fR.
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR
Per-host probe timeout, > 0. Default: 1.0. Applied to the ping (rounded up
to whole seconds on Linux), to each TCP connect, and to the DNS lookup.
.TP
\fB\-w\fR, \fB\-\-workers\fR \fICOUNT\fR
Maximum concurrent probes, 1\(en256. Default: 64.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-rate\fR \fIPER_SECOND\fR
Maximum addresses started per second; \fB0\fR means unlimited. Default: 50.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-hosts\fR \fICOUNT\fR
Refuse networks with more probe addresses than this. Default: 1024.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-large\fR
Scan even if the network exceeds \fB\-\-max\-hosts\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-routable\fR
Scan even if the network is globally routable address space.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-ports\fR \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated TCP ports (1\(en65535) to try, e.g. \fB22,80,443\fR, if a
host does not answer ICMP ping, such as one behind a firewall that filters it.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-dns\fR
Skip reverse DNS lookups; report IP addresses only.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print results as a JSON array of
.B {"ip": ..., "hostname": ...}
objects instead of a table.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help and exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Print the package version and exit (from
.IR src/mu2edaq_cluster_tools/_version.py ,
the same source as
.B ssh\-selector \-\-version
and the package metadata).
.SH CONFIGURATION PRECEDENCE
For every option, the effective value is chosen in this order:
commandline flag, then environment variable, then the \fB\-\-config\fR
YAML file, then the built-in default. An empty environment variable is
treated as unset. The configuration file is read only when
.B \-\-config
is given; there is no default path.
Recognised keys are
.BR network ,
.BR timeout ,
.BR workers ,
.BR rate ,
.BR max_hosts ,
.BR allow_large ,
.BR allow_routable ,
.B ports
(list or comma-separated string) and
.BR no_dns .
The boolean flags
.BR \-\-allow\-large ,
.B \-\-allow\-routable
and
.B \-\-no\-dns
can only switch a behaviour on from the commandline; to switch one off when
the file sets it, set the environment variable to
.BR 0 .
.SH FILES
lan-scan profiles live in
.IR config/lan\-scan/ ,
not
.IR config/ ,
because
.B ssh\-selector
offers every
.I *.yaml
in
.I ./
and
.I ./config/
as a host file.
.TP
.I config/lan\-scan/lan_scan\-mu2edaq.yaml
Shipped profile:
.BR 131.225.245.0/24 ,
.B site-routable
(requires \fB\-\-allow\-routable\fR), ICMP only (no TCP ports), 16 workers,
20 addresses/s.
.TP
.I config/lan\-scan/lan_scan\-mu2edaq\-data.yaml
Shipped profile:
.BR 10.226.9.0/24 ,
ports 22, 80, 443.
.TP
.I config/lan\-scan/lan_scan\-mu2edaq\-ipmi.yaml
Shipped profile:
.BR 192.168.246.0/24 ,
ports 22, 80, 443.
.TP
.I config/lan\-scan/lan_scan.yaml.example
Annotated template for a new scan profile.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Booleans accept 1/true/yes/on and 0/false/no/off.
.TP
.B LAN_SCAN_NETWORK
Network to scan (same syntax as \fB\-\-network\fR).
.TP
.B LAN_SCAN_TIMEOUT
Per-host probe timeout in seconds.
.TP
.B LAN_SCAN_WORKERS
Maximum concurrent probes.
.TP
.B LAN_SCAN_RATE
Maximum addresses started per second (0 = unlimited).
.TP
.B LAN_SCAN_MAX_HOSTS
Network size limit.
.TP
.B LAN_SCAN_ALLOW_LARGE
Boolean; as \fB\-\-allow\-large\fR.
.TP
.B LAN_SCAN_ALLOW_ROUTABLE
Boolean; as \fB\-\-allow\-routable\fR.
.TP
.B LAN_SCAN_PORTS
TCP fallback port list (same syntax as \fB\-\-ports\fR).
.TP
.B LAN_SCAN_NO_DNS
Boolean; as \fB\-\-no\-dns\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Scan completed (regardless of how many hosts responded).
.TP
.B 1
No network given and auto-detection found none, or found more than one.
.TP
.B 2
Usage or configuration error: an invalid value from the commandline,
the environment or the config file (bad CIDR, non-numeric or out-of-range
timeout, worker count, rate, size limit or port, unrecognised boolean), or a
missing, unreadable or malformed \fB\-\-config\fR file.
.TP
.B 3
Scan refused: the network exceeds \fB\-\-max\-hosts\fR without
\fB\-\-allow\-large\fR, or is globally routable without
\fB\-\-allow\-routable\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
Scan the local subnet (only when the host has exactly one):
.PP
.RS
.B lan\-scan
.RE
.PP
Scan a specific subnet, skipping DNS lookups:
.PP
.RS
.B lan\-scan \-\-network 10.0.0.0/24 \-\-no\-dns
.RE
.PP
Also try common TCP ports for hosts that filter ICMP, and emit JSON:
.PP
.RS
.B lan\-scan \-n 10.226.9.0/24 \-\-ports 22,80,443 \-\-json
.RE
.PP
Use a shipped profile with gentler concurrency and rate:
.PP
.RS
.B lan\-scan \-c config/lan\-scan/lan_scan\-mu2edaq\-data.yaml \-w 16 \-r 10
.RE
.PP
Scan the routable Mu2e subnet (authorised operators only):
.PP
.RS
.B lan\-scan \-c config/lan\-scan/lan_scan\-mu2edaq.yaml \-\-allow\-routable
.RE
.PP
Scan a /20 deliberately:
.PP
.RS
.B lan\-scan \-n 10.226.0.0/20 \-\-allow\-large
.RE
.SH BUGS
On Linux, timeouts below one second are rounded up to one second for the
ping probe (older iputils accept whole seconds only).
.PP
An abandoned reverse-DNS lookup keeps its daemon thread until the resolver
returns; it does not delay the scan or process exit.
.PP
IPv6 is not scanned.
.PP
See
.I docs/ISSUES\-PENDING.md
in the source tree.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh\-selector (1),
.BR ping (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Andrew Norman, Mu2e DAQ Group, Fermi National Accelerator Laboratory.
